1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,549
Two hands shake.

2
00:00:01,549 --> 00:00:03,435
Two strips of hook tape stick.

3
00:00:03,435 --> 00:00:05,275
A bicycle chain grips a gear.

4
00:00:05,675 --> 00:00:08,665
Each grip works only where
the two parts overlap.

5
00:00:09,115 --> 00:00:11,508
Aarav sees it while fixing his bicycle.

6
00:00:11,508 --> 00:00:15,509
The chain holds the gear only where
the teeth sit inside the links.

7
00:00:15,859 --> 00:00:18,906
More teeth inside the
links, a firmer grip.

8
00:00:19,406 --> 00:00:21,549
Atoms hold each other the same way.

9
00:00:21,549 --> 00:00:25,426
By the end, you will know why some
bonds grip harder than others.

10
00:00:26,076 --> 00:00:28,696
So how do two atoms hold on to each other?

11
00:00:29,046 --> 00:00:30,951
Lewis said atoms share
a pair of electrons.

12
00:00:30,951 --> 00:00:32,445
But where do those electrons sit?

13
00:00:32,795 --> 00:00:34,575
Valence bond theory answers that.

14
00:00:34,575 --> 00:00:36,533
Heitler and London gave it in 1927.

15
00:00:36,533 --> 00:00:38,281
Pauling and others built on it.

16
00:00:38,631 --> 00:00:42,449
An orbital is the space where an
electron is most likely to be.

17
00:00:42,449 --> 00:00:44,660
Think of it as the electron's cloud.

18
00:00:45,010 --> 00:00:49,754
The big idea: a bond forms when an
orbital of one atom overlaps an orbital

19
00:00:49,754 --> 00:00:50,833
of the other.

20
00:00:51,233 --> 00:00:55,383
Inside the overlap, two electrons
with opposite spins pair up, and both

21
00:00:55,383 --> 00:00:56,751
nuclei pull on them.

22
00:00:57,401 --> 00:01:00,743
Let's watch two hydrogen
atoms on our molecule bench.

23
00:01:00,743 --> 00:01:03,483
Far apart, they do not feel each other.

24
00:01:03,833 --> 00:01:06,222
As they come closer, new pulls start.

25
00:01:06,222 --> 00:01:08,838
Each nucleus pulls the
other atom's electron.

26
00:01:09,238 --> 00:01:10,626
Pushes start too.

27
00:01:10,626 --> 00:01:12,340
Nucleus pushes nucleus.

28
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:14,163
Electron pushes electron.

29
00:01:14,563 --> 00:01:19,582
At first the pulls win, so the energy
goes down, like a ball rolling into

30
00:01:19,582 --> 00:01:20,482
a valley.

31
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:24,024
At seventy four picometres,
the energy is the lowest.

32
00:01:24,024 --> 00:01:27,225
A picometre is a millionth
of a millionth of a metre.

33
00:01:27,225 --> 00:01:29,069
Here pulls and pushes balance.

34
00:01:29,069 --> 00:01:31,101
This distance is the bond length.

35
00:01:31,501 --> 00:01:33,932
Push them closer, and the pushes win.

36
00:01:33,932 --> 00:01:35,360
The energy shoots up.

37
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:40,010
At the bottom of the valley, four hundred
and thirty five point eight kilojoules

38
00:01:40,010 --> 00:01:41,322
per mole is released.

39
00:01:41,322 --> 00:01:44,175
To break the bond, we
must put that energy back.

40
00:01:44,175 --> 00:01:45,751
That is the bond enthalpy.

41
00:01:46,401 --> 00:01:47,829
Now the overlap itself.

42
00:01:47,829 --> 00:01:51,419
Orbitals have signs, like the
crest and the trough of a wave.

43
00:01:51,819 --> 00:01:54,357
Same signs meet: positive overlap.

44
00:01:54,357 --> 00:01:55,524
A bond forms.

45
00:01:55,924 --> 00:01:58,819
Opposite signs meet: negative overlap.

46
00:01:58,819 --> 00:01:59,719
No bond.

47
00:02:00,094 --> 00:02:04,828
Or they meet at a right angle, and
the parts cancel out: zero overlap.

48
00:02:04,828 --> 00:02:05,728
No bond.

49
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:08,586
Positive overlap comes in two kinds.

50
00:02:08,586 --> 00:02:13,402
Head-on, along the line joining
the two nuclei, gives a sigma bond.

51
00:02:13,402 --> 00:02:16,990
It can be s with s, s with p, or p with p.

52
00:02:17,390 --> 00:02:21,982
Side by side, with two p orbitals
standing parallel, gives a pi bond.

53
00:02:21,982 --> 00:02:24,709
Its cloud sits above and below that line.

54
00:02:25,109 --> 00:02:26,366
Pause and think.

55
00:02:26,366 --> 00:02:29,991
Which grips harder, head-on
sigma or side by side pi?

56
00:02:33,191 --> 00:02:34,091
Sigma!

57
00:02:33,822 --> 00:02:38,496
Head-on overlap is bigger, and a
bigger overlap means a stronger bond.

58
00:02:39,146 --> 00:02:41,759
Let's build the rules, one link at a time.

59
00:02:42,109 --> 00:02:43,009
Link one.

60
00:02:42,916 --> 00:02:45,749
A bond needs overlap, with the same sign.

61
00:02:46,499 --> 00:02:47,399
Link two.

62
00:02:47,287 --> 00:02:50,294
The bigger the overlap,
the stronger the bond.

63
00:02:51,044 --> 00:02:51,944
Link three.

64
00:02:51,923 --> 00:02:55,464
The first bond between two
atoms is always a sigma bond.

65
00:02:56,214 --> 00:02:57,114
Link four.

66
00:02:57,093 --> 00:02:59,859
A double bond is one sigma plus one pi.

67
00:02:59,859 --> 00:03:02,625
A triple bond is one sigma plus two pi.

68
00:03:03,375 --> 00:03:04,275
Link five.

69
00:03:04,175 --> 00:03:07,689
A pi bond forms only where a
sigma bond is already there.

70
00:03:08,439 --> 00:03:09,546
Remember this.

71
00:03:09,546 --> 00:03:11,193
Bonds grip by overlap.

72
00:03:11,193 --> 00:03:14,227
Head-on gives sigma,
side by side gives pi.

73
00:03:14,927 --> 00:03:16,832
Now, one worked example.

74
00:03:16,832 --> 00:03:20,791
Count the sigma and pi bonds
in ethene, C two H four.

75
00:03:21,091 --> 00:03:21,991
Step one.

76
00:03:21,790 --> 00:03:22,690
Draw it.

77
00:03:22,447 --> 00:03:24,331
Two carbons share a double bond.

78
00:03:24,331 --> 00:03:26,429
Each carbon also holds two hydrogens.

79
00:03:27,179 --> 00:03:28,079
Step two.

80
00:03:27,999 --> 00:03:30,158
Every single bond is one sigma.

81
00:03:30,158 --> 00:03:32,978
Four carbon hydrogen
bonds give four sigma.

82
00:03:33,728 --> 00:03:34,628
Step three.

83
00:03:34,588 --> 00:03:37,386
The double bond gives
one sigma plus one pi.

84
00:03:38,136 --> 00:03:39,036
Step four.

85
00:03:38,994 --> 00:03:40,009
Add them up.

86
00:03:40,009 --> 00:03:42,297
Five sigma bonds and one pi bond.

87
00:03:42,947 --> 00:03:45,384
Now put sigma and pi side by side.

88
00:03:45,784 --> 00:03:47,689
Sigma is head-on overlap.

89
00:03:47,689 --> 00:03:49,380
Pi is side by side.

90
00:03:49,780 --> 00:03:52,525
Sigma's cloud sits on the
line between the nuclei.

91
00:03:52,525 --> 00:03:54,532
Pi's cloud sits above and below it.

92
00:03:54,932 --> 00:03:57,067
Sigma is the stronger bond.

93
00:03:57,067 --> 00:03:58,280
Pi is weaker.

94
00:03:58,680 --> 00:04:00,286
Sigma can stand alone.

95
00:04:00,286 --> 00:04:02,366
Pi always comes with a sigma.

96
00:04:03,016 --> 00:04:04,477
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:04:04,827 --> 00:04:08,807
A triple bond has three bonds,
so students count three pi bonds.

98
00:04:09,207 --> 00:04:10,107
Wrong.

99
00:04:09,790 --> 00:04:11,713
One of them is always sigma.

100
00:04:11,713 --> 00:04:14,286
A triple bond is one sigma and two pi.

101
00:04:14,936 --> 00:04:16,406
Where is this asked?

102
00:04:16,406 --> 00:04:18,084
Both JEE Main and NEET.

103
00:04:18,084 --> 00:04:22,278
Counting sigma and pi bonds, and
statements about orbital overlap.

104
00:04:22,878 --> 00:04:24,116
Exam level now.

105
00:04:24,116 --> 00:04:27,863
Propyne is CH three,
joined to C triple bond CH.

106
00:04:27,863 --> 00:04:30,843
How many sigma and pi bonds does it have?

107
00:04:30,843 --> 00:04:32,134
Pause and solve.

108
00:04:37,534 --> 00:04:38,938
The answer is A.

109
00:04:38,938 --> 00:04:40,679
Six sigma and two pi.

110
00:04:41,179 --> 00:04:43,425
Count the carbon hydrogen bonds.

111
00:04:43,425 --> 00:04:45,201
Three plus one is four.

112
00:04:45,201 --> 00:04:46,653
Each is one sigma.

113
00:04:47,153 --> 00:04:50,681
The carbon carbon single
bond adds one more sigma.

114
00:04:50,681 --> 00:04:51,785
Five so far.

115
00:04:52,285 --> 00:04:55,195
The triple bond gives
one sigma and two pi.

116
00:04:55,195 --> 00:04:57,284
Total, six sigma and two pi.

117
00:04:57,284 --> 00:04:59,838
B treats the whole triple bond as pi.

118
00:05:00,438 --> 00:05:01,978
Here is your recap.

119
00:05:01,978 --> 00:05:03,561
Screenshot this card.

120
00:05:06,761 --> 00:05:08,947
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

121
00:05:09,347 --> 00:05:10,247
One.

122
00:05:09,822 --> 00:05:14,332
How much energy is released when two
hydrogen atoms form one mole of bonds?

123
00:05:15,132 --> 00:05:16,032
Two.

124
00:05:15,586 --> 00:05:18,302
Why is a sigma bond
stronger than a pi bond?

125
00:05:19,102 --> 00:05:20,002
Three.

126
00:05:19,621 --> 00:05:22,785
How many sigma and pi bonds
are there in a triple bond?

127
00:05:23,685 --> 00:05:26,452
Next time: carbon has only
two half filled orbitals.

128
00:05:26,452 --> 00:05:28,497
So how does it make four equal bonds?

129
00:05:28,497 --> 00:05:30,082
We find out in hybridisation.
